    {
      "title": "Dana stores the key in an AWS profile",
      "before": [
        "The AWS command line program `aws` stores a key under a profile name. The name must be `pi-memory-person-dana`, because the person config names it.",
        "Dana pastes the two parts of the key when `aws configure` asks for them."
      ],
      "frame": {
        "kind": "shell",
        "title": "dana@laptop: ~",
        "source": "the [AWS CLI documentation of `aws configure`](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html)",
        "live": false,
        "lines": [
          {
            "t": "cmd",
            "prompt": "dana@laptop:~$",
            "text": "aws configure --profile pi-memory-person-dana"
          },
          {
            "t": "ask",
            "text": "AWS Access Key ID [None]: ",
            "answer": "<access key id>"
          },
          {
            "t": "ask",
            "text": "AWS Secret Access Key [None]: ",
            "answer": "<secret access key>"
          },
          {
            "t": "ask",
            "text": "Default region name [None]: ",
            "answer": "us-east-1"
          },
          {
            "t": "ask",
            "text": "Default output format [None]: ",
            "answer": "json"
          },
          {
            "t": "cmd",
            "prompt": "dana@laptop:~$",
            "text": "chmod 600 ~/.aws/credentials"
          }
        ]
      },
      "after": [
        "The key is now in `~/.aws/credentials`, in the section `[pi-memory-person-dana]`. The region goes to `~/.aws/config`.",
        "`chmod 600` lets only Dana read and write the file."
      ]
